A||||12||2M4 Dale||Everything is new !!! Still ain't right||||9||
Z||000000||2M4 Dale||02-08-2000||12:39 AM||||OK Guys, I've replaced everything in my hydraulic clutch system. Master/slave/and new hydraulic line. I've bled the system. My car is an 87 coupe 4cly./5spd. The clutch pedal is steel. I'm still only getting 7/8" to 15/16" of travel at the tranny. This is supposed to be minimum of 1"...RIGHT ??? What do I try next ? At this rate I won't be ready for the MID-OHIO MEET @#$%@||||reg||9
Z||000001||TOM||02-08-2000||12:56 AM||||Is the clutch pedal push rod adjusted right?||||reg||1
Z||000002||2M4 Dale||02-08-2000||06:29 AM||||I don't know...How do you adjust it ?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||02-08-2000||08:07 AM||||Hey Dale,<BR>Have you tried gravity bleeding yet. It has worked for me every time, and it is only a one man job.<P>Fill the m.c. to the top, then twist the bleeder screw counter clockwise about 2 turns. Fluid should start to come out on its own. Just keep the m.c. full. After several minutes close 'er up, and try it out.<BR>I don't know of any adjustments you can make to the pedal push rod, TOM would have to explain that.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-08-2000||09:12 AM||||Doesn't the master cyl rod have to be installed a particular way? There's a change in how it hooks up depending on what pedal/rod/cyl you have. I don't rememeber right now what it was. but I recall that if it's wrong the clutch doesn't work right. I think it also binds the master cyl if it's wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000005||pollock||02-08-2000||09:28 AM||||V-8 Archie recommends this technique.  After bleeding the system normally, get a friend to help you do this part.  Remove the slave bleeder screw completely.  Jam the rod all the way in by hand and have your buddy put the screw back in and tighten it up.  Then release the rod.  ||||reg||3
Z||000006||William Federle||02-08-2000||01:48 PM||||And before you jam the slave cylinder in be sure and put a rag or something over the opening or you'll get an eye full of brake fluid - it stings.<P>The end of the master cylinder shaft is bigger on one side than the other. The big side should point up - like a lower case 'b'<BR>lying on its back.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||02-08-2000||08:56 PM||||Bet it doesnt sting as bad as Skydrol. Thats the hydralic fluid used in big planes. And it is corrosive||||reg||1
Z||000008||2M4 Dale||02-09-2000||06:55 AM||||I think I still have air in the lines/slave cylinder. A friend has one of those power bleeder deals...He's gonna bring it over tonight and see what we can do with that. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||02-10-2000||07:32 PM||||Yeah,the mechanics I know mentioned the importance of washing hands before going to the bathroom after handling Skydrol. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>What made a difference on my car was replacing the worn bushing that the pedal pivots on;just enough to make the thing work again.||||reg||1
Z||000010||2M4 Dale||02-11-2000||06:27 AM||||Well, I've got the air out and everything in the hydraulic system seems OK. I think the problem is that the  1.throwout bearing is toast 2.the pressure plate is stuck to the clutch 3. ????....There is no pressure on the clutch fork lever at all. You can push and pull it back and forth with 0 effort. Is it time to drop the engine ???||||reg||9
Z||000011||fiero56||02-11-2000||10:38 AM||||Sounds like it could be one of several things. 1. the pressure plate could be really bad; 2. the clutch fork could have a break somewhere on it, allowing the shaft to move freely; 3. the t/o bearing could have  somehow slipped out of place, is sitting at the bottom of the tranny, and therefore there is room for the clutch fork to move freely. <BR>Any one of these will require pulling the tranny from the engine. Whichever method you prefer.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||2M4 Dale||02-11-2000||11:51 AM||||JOY JOY JOY out the engine comes !!! At least I have a little time before nice weather eh'||||reg||1
